# 02. Active Directory Domain Services

## Overview

This lab demonstrates the deployment and configuration of **Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)** in a Windows Server environment using **Oracle VirtualBox**.

The lab includes creating a Domain Controller, configuring an Active Directory domain, creating Organizational Units and user accounts, managing security groups, joining a Windows client to the domain, and testing Active Directory functionality.

## Lab Environment

* Oracle VirtualBox
* Windows Server
* Windows Client
* Active Directory Domain Services
* Domain Controller
* VirtualBox internal/lab network

## Objectives

* Install and configure Active Directory Domain Services
* Promote Windows Server to a Domain Controller
* Create an Active Directory domain
* Create Organizational Units (OUs)
* Create and manage user accounts
* Create and manage security groups
* Configure computer accounts
* Join a Windows client computer to the domain
* Manage Active Directory using administrative tools
* Use PowerShell to manage Active Directory
* Verify domain functionality

## Skills Demonstrated

* Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)
* Domain Controller deployment
* Domain creation
* Organizational Units (OUs)
* User account management
* Security groups
* Computer accounts
* Domain joining
* Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC)
* Active Directory Administrative Center
* Active Directory Sites and Services
* Active Directory PowerShell
* Group Policy

## Lab Steps

### 1. Install Active Directory Domain Services

* Open Server Manager
* Select **Add Roles and Features**
* Install the **Active Directory Domain Services** role
* Verify the AD DS role installation

### 2. Promote the Server to a Domain Controller

* Open the AD DS configuration wizard
* Select **Add a new forest**
* Create the Active Directory domain
* Configure DNS settings
* Configure the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) password
* Complete the Domain Controller promotion
* Restart the server

### 3. Verify the Domain Controller

* Log into the newly created domain
* Open Server Manager
* Verify the AD DS role
* Open Active Directory Users and Computers
* Confirm that the domain was successfully created

### 4. Create Organizational Units

Create Organizational Units to organize users and computers within Active Directory.

Example:

```text
Domain
├── Users
├── Computers
├── IT
├── HR
├── Finance
└── Management
```

### 5. Create User Accounts

* Create test user accounts
* Assign users to the appropriate Organizational Units
* Configure usernames and passwords
* Verify the accounts in Active Directory Users and Computers

### 6. Create Security Groups

* Create security groups for different departments
* Add users to the appropriate security groups
* Verify group membership
* Test access based on group membership

### 7. Configure Computer Accounts

* Verify computer accounts in Active Directory
* Organize computer accounts into the appropriate Organizational Units
* Confirm that domain-joined computers appear in Active Directory

### 8. Join a Windows Client to the Domain

* Configure the client computer to use the Domain Controller as its DNS server
* Open Windows System Properties
* Select the option to join the computer to a domain
* Enter the Active Directory domain name
* Provide domain administrator credentials
* Restart the client computer
* Log into the client using a domain account

### 9. Manage Active Directory

The following administrative tools were used:

* Active Directory Users and Computers
* Active Directory Administrative Center
* Active Directory Sites and Services
* Server Manager
* Active Directory PowerShell

### 10. Test Active Directory

Example commands used to verify the environment:

```powershell
whoami
hostname
ipconfig /all
nslookup <your-domain>
```

PowerShell Active Directory commands:

```powershell
Get-ADUser -Filter *
Get-ADComputer -Filter *
Get-ADGroup -Filter *
Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter *
```

## What I Learned

This lab provided hands-on experience building a functional **Active Directory domain environment** from the ground up.

I learned how Active Directory relies on **DNS, networking, authentication, Organizational Units, security groups, and Group Policy** to manage users and computers in a Windows domain environment.
